{
  "image_name": "1b8d010d7e1bdd0e7357796de1f37dca.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how T‑cell receptor signaling is coupled to the CARD11–BCL10–MALT1 signaling hub to activate inflammatory gene expression. Signals from the TCR and the costimulatory receptor CD28 feed into kinases such as PI3K, PDK1, AKT, and PKCθ, which converge on CARD11 at the membrane. CARD11 appears to undergo activating phosphorylation and then acts as a scaffold that recruits BCL‑10 and MALT1, forming the CBM complex.\n\nDownstream of this complex, several ubiquitin-dependent steps are highlighted. BCL‑10 and associated factors recruit cIAP1/2, LUBAC, TRAF6, and TAK1/TAB proteins, generating ubiquitin chains and activating the IKK complex. IKK then phosphorylates IκBα, leading to its degradation by the proteasome and releasing NF‑κB to enter the nucleus and drive transcription. Parallel branches also activate JNK and p38, indicating broader MAP kinase signaling.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes MALT1 as both a scaffold and a protease, with a labeled proteolytic activity branch. On the right, glutamine uptake through the transporter ASCT2 is connected to this signaling assembly, suggesting metabolic input into CBM activation. Overall, the image presents the CBM complex as a central organizer linking receptor stimulation, ubiquitination, kinase cascades, and NF‑κB activation.",
